Here is a jQuery solution that detects when a user attempts to access your site using Internet Explorer mode (IE mode) within Microsoft Edge and automatically redirects them to standard Edge mode:

```javascript
$(document).ready(function() {
    // Check if running in IE mode within Edge
    var isIE = !!document.documentMode;
    var isEdge = navigator.userAgent.indexOf('Edg/') !== -1;

    if (isIE && isEdge) {
        // User is in IE mode - force redirect to standard Edge
        var currentUrl = window.location.href;

        // Use microsoft-edge protocol to open in standard mode
        window.location.href = 'microsoft-edge:' + currentUrl;

        // Fallback for cases where protocol handler doesn't work
        setTimeout(function() {
            $('body').html(
                '<div style="padding: 20px; font-family: Arial;">' +
                '<h2>Browser Compatibility Required</h2>' +
                '<p>This modern web <strong>technology</strong> requires Microsoft Edge (standard mode).</p>' +
                '<p>Please disable Internet Explorer mode for this site or <a href="microsoft-edge:' + 
                currentUrl + '">click here</a> to open in Edge.</p>' +
                '</div>'
            );
        }, 1000);
    }
});
```

**How it works:**
- `document.documentMode` detects IE rendering engine (present in both old IE and IE mode in Edge)
- `navigator.userAgent` checks for "Edg/" to confirm it's Edge browser in IE mode
- The `microsoft-edge:` protocol forces the URL to open in standard Edge mode instead of IE mode

**Alternative for actual Internet Explorer users:**
If you also need to catch users on legacy Internet Explorer (not just IE mode in Edge), remove the `isEdge` check to redirect all IE users to Edge using the same protocol handler.

